


Implement METHOD-level XML parameter loading
using the existing INVOCATION configuration scope.

SUITE, TEST and CLASS XML parameter loading
are already implemented and tested.

Requirements:

1. Obtain local method parameters from the
   matching XmlInclude.getLocalParameters().

2. Reuse the existing framework parameter
   loader, context registry and layer store.

3. Register the FRAMEWORK_PARAMETERS layer
   on the correct INVOCATION context.

4. Preserve inheritance from CLASS, TEST
   and SUITE contexts.

5. METHOD parameters override parent XML
   parameters within the same source priority.

6. Preserve PROGRAMMATIC and EXPLICIT_OVERRIDE
   source precedence.

7. Ensure @BeforeMethod, @Test and @AfterMethod
   observe the same effective method configuration.

8. Ensure method parameters are available
   before the corresponding @BeforeMethod body
   starts, not only when @Test starts.

9. Inspect the actual TestNG 7.12.0 lifecycle
   information used to identify the upcoming
   test method. Do not confuse configuration
   methods with the target test method.

10. Match the correct XmlInclude within the
    owning XmlClass and XmlTest.

11. Never silently choose an ambiguous method
    mapping or an incorrect XML test.

12. Preserve independent INVOCATION contexts
    for parallel DataProvider executions,
    repeated invocations and retries.

13. Never duplicate layers or copy inherited
    values into the method layer.

14. Preserve cleanup, thread isolation and
    existing lifecycle safety guarantees.

15. Do not modify the configuration resolver,
    legacy properties manager or listeners.

Add real TestNG integration tests verifying:

- Different methods with different XML values.
- METHOD precedence over CLASS, TEST and SUITE.
- Correct configuration in @BeforeMethod,
  @Test and @AfterMethod.
- Parallel DataProvider executions.
- Retry behavior.
- No configuration leakage between methods.
- No duplicate or orphan layers.

Run the relevant tests and the complete
TestNG module.

If the current lifecycle integration cannot
identify the target method reliably before
@BeforeMethod, report the exact blocker
instead of implementing a partial solution.

Keep production changes small and focused.









